######## INVESTIGATION ROUND 3 — mismatched port pair ########
Terrence moved the MEMBER 1 end 1.0.25 -> 1.0.26 (identified from the hardware, not
from the verbal report: port1.0.26 came up 10GBASE-SR/a-10G while port2.0.25 stayed
connected). Resulting pair is DELIBERATELY MISMATCHED: port1.0.26 <-> port2.0.25.
No reboot needed - port1.0.26's `stackport` had already been cleared in round 2.

  pair                    media          numbers      m1 TX   m2 RX        m2 TX  m2 Status
  ----------------------  -------------  -----------  ------  -----------  -----  ---------
  port1.0.7 <-> 2.0.7     1000BASE-SX 1G matched         409  424 clean        0  Failed
  port1.0.25 <-> 2.0.25   10GBASE-SR 10G matched         393  408 clean        0  Failed
  port1.0.26 <-> 2.0.25   10GBASE-SR 10G MISMATCHED      492  1086 clean       0  Failed

=> Matching-pair hypothesis EXCLUDED. Three independent port pairs across two media
   types, two speeds, and both matched and mismatched port numbering all reproduce
   the identical signature: master transmits healthchecks, backup receives every one
   of them error-free, backup transmits nothing, backup reports "Failed".

CUMULATIVE EXCLUSIONS: cabling, optics, fibre pair, pluggable modules, media type,
   link speed, port number, matched-vs-mismatched numbering, ASIC port, STP blocking,
   VLAN choice, config content/ordering, config save, config sync to member 2,
   stackport interference, transient/settling, and full stack reboot (x2).
REMAINING VARIABLE: the backup member itself - unit 264A23052 specifically, or the
   backup ROLE on this build. Discriminator = master swap (reboot member 1).

