*** BENCH DOC UPDATE — TESTBOX-ACCESS.md section 4a IS NOW STALE ***
That section records (2026-07-30): on the IE520, port 27/28 are dedicated stackports
where "`no stackport` is accepted and saved, but the flag RETURNS AFTER REBOOT on the
real member's ports". On this build that did NOT reproduce. `no stackport` on
port1.0.27 and port2.0.28 SURVIVED the reboot cleanly:
    show running-config interface port1.0.27  ->  switchport / switchport mode access
    show running-config interface port2.0.28  ->  switchport / switchport mode access
    show interface status: port1.0.27 + port2.0.28 = connected, vlan 1 (NOT stackport)
    stack now runs on the single pair: "Stack port1.0.28 status Learnt neighbor 2,
    connected port2.0.27";  Operational Status "Normal operation"
Either the behaviour changed in this software build, or the 2026-07-30 observation had
another cause. Re-verify before relying on either statement.

4th PORT PAIR RESULT — resiliency link on port1.0.27 <-> port2.0.28 (1xCOPPER PAS 10G):
    port1.0.27 (member 1, MASTER) : output 408 (395 multicast)
    port2.0.28 (member 2, BACKUP) : input  422 (409 multicast), output 17
    member 2 Status = Failed        (member 1 = Configured, correct for master)
  The 17 reverse packets are not healthcheck replies - a working link would reply at
  the master's send rate (~409), not 4% of it.

CUMULATIVE PORT-PAIR EVIDENCE (all four reproduce the identical signature):
  1. port1.0.7  <-> port2.0.7   1000BASE-SX   1G   matched numbering
  2. port1.0.25 <-> port2.0.25  10GBASE-SR   10G   matched numbering
  3. port1.0.26 <-> port2.0.25  10GBASE-SR   10G   MISMATCHED numbering
  4. port1.0.27 <-> port2.0.28  1xCOPPER PAS 10G   dedicated stackport hardware
=> Port hardware is now COMPLETELY excluded: three distinct PHY/media types including
   the dedicated stacking PHYs, two speeds, matched and mismatched numbering.

